<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Catalyst.entitlements PLUS the Keychain group — used only when a
  Developer ID provisioning profile is embedded in the bundle.

  Read the long comment in Catalyst.entitlements first. The short version:
  keychain-access-groups is a RESTRICTED entitlement. macOS validates it
  against a profile inside the bundle, and a build with no profile is not
  merely denied the Keychain — it is SIGKILLed before main() with
  "Restricted entitlements not validated, bailing out". That is why this
  is a separate file rather than a couple of extra keys in the other one:
  the wrong one of the two produces an app that does not start, and the
  choice between them is made by scripts/build-catalyst.sh from whether a
  profile is actually there to authorise it.

  With this file in play the journal, the fasting log, the coordinates
  and the sync identity go back to the Keychain, and durableWrite.ts stops
  keeping its plaintext copy — see the fallback note there for what this
  is worth.

  Every value is spelled literally. `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)` and
  `$(CFBundleIdentifier)` are build-time expansions and this file is fed
  straight to codesign, so nothing expands. The identifier carries the
  maccatalyst prefix that DERIVE_MACCATALYST_PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER
  adds — it is not the iOS one.

  These are not guesses. They are what the Catalyst profile Xcode had
  already made on this machine actually authorises:

    application-identifier              GAW23HT439.maccatalyst.com.hassan.prayerapp
    com.apple.application-identifier    the same
    com.apple.developer.team-identifier GAW23HT439
    keychain-access-groups              GAW23HT439.* and com.apple.token
    application-groups                  group.com.prayerapp

  Two things follow. The Keychain group below is covered by that
  wildcard, so it needs no capability enabled anywhere — which is why
  there is no Keychain Sharing checkbox to hunt for in the developer
  portal, and why trying to register the App ID by hand fails: Xcode
  registered it long ago, so the portal reports the identifier as taken.

  And the profile names the app group WITHOUT the team prefix while this
  file keeps it, because the prefixed spelling is the one arrived at by
  experiment and it has never failed; see Catalyst.entitlements. macOS
  reads them as the same group. If a build ever refuses to launch, that
  difference is the first thing to suspect and the profile's spelling is
  the thing to try.

  com.apple.application-identifier is here because on macOS that is what
  the data-protection Keychain uses as an app's default access group.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>GAW23HT439.group.com.prayerapp</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
	<string>GAW23HT439.maccatalyst.com.hassan.prayerapp</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
	<string>GAW23HT439</string>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>GAW23HT439.maccatalyst.com.hassan.prayerapp</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
